        isFree: true,
        body: "If I had known what I know now when I arrived, everything would have been different.\n\nNot easier, necessarily. But different. More intentional. Less wasted energy. Fewer decisions made in the dark because nobody had thought to turn the lights on.\n\nI’ve been thinking about that a lot lately.\n\nI’m in my senior year now, and I have the strange experience of watching freshmen walk across this same campus with the same look I had four years ago — a mix of excitement and quiet panic, trying to seem like they know where they’re going when most of them have no idea. I was that person. I remember exactly what it felt like.\n\n> I also remember what nobody told me. Nobody told me that getting in was the easy part.\n\nNot easy — I know what it cost to get here. The applications, the tests, the visa interviews, the conversations where you had to convince someone you were worth the investment. The family dinners where the weight of everyone’s expectations sat at the table with you. The moment you actually packed your bags and understood, maybe for the first time, that you were really doing this.\n\n> That was hard. And you did it.\n\nBut arriving is the beginning of a different game entirely. And this game has rules that nobody hands you. No orientation packet covers them. No professor mentions them in the first lecture. You figure them out on your own — slowly, expensively, through a series of mistakes that cost you time and energy you didn’t know you were spending.\n\nThat’s how I learned most of what’s in this book. The hard way.\n\nI made good decisions in college. I worked hard. I showed up. I built something I’m proud of. But I also carried a thought I’ve never been able to shake: I figured some of this out too late.\n\nSome of the things I’m about to tell you — things that now seem so obvious I can barely believe I didn’t know them — I only understood in my junior or senior year. By then, some windows had already closed. Some opportunities I could have had freshman year were gone. Time I could have spent building something was spent going in circles because I didn’t know what I was supposed to be building.\n\n> That’s the honest version. And you deserve the honest version.\n\nThis isn’t a guide in the traditional sense. I’m not going to give you a checklist of things to do or a list of life hacks to optimize your GPA. This is the conversation I wish someone had sat me down for the week I arrived — direct, specific, and without any of the polished nonsense that tends to show up in books like this.\n\nI’m going to tell you what the first months actually feel like, and why most international students go through a version of the same shock. I’m going to tell you how the American university system actually works — not the way it’s described in the brochure, but the way students who do well in it learn to use it. I’m going to tell you about the social choices that compound over four years, and about the career decisions that seem far away freshman year but are being made right now whether you’re thinking about them or not.\n\nI’m going to tell you about money, and time, and the quiet pressure of being far from home, and the way your identity can quietly erode if you’re not paying attention. About resume building and internship searching and the visa timelines that will affect your life in ways you haven’t fully considered yet. All of it. Without pretending any of it is simpler than it is.\n\nBecause the truth is that most international students walk through college on information delay. They find out about office hours in sophomore year. They figure out how to use their alumni network junior year. They understand what CPT and OPT actually mean sometime around when they need to use them, which is already almost too late to plan.\n\nMeanwhile, the students who move fastest aren’t always the smartest or the most talented. They’re the most informed. They made the same decisions earlier — with more clarity, less guesswork, and a much better understanding of what each choice was actually worth.\n\nThat gap — between what you know and what you could know — is exactly what this book is trying to close.\n\n> You already did one of the hardest things: you got here.\n\nYou left what was familiar. You crossed the kind of distance, geographic and otherwise, that most people never cross. You showed up somewhere new with the intention of building something real.\n\n> Now the question is what you do with it.\n\nI wrote this so that at the end of your senior year, when you’re the one looking back, you feel like you used this time. That you made the decisions you’d make again. That you didn’t find out what you should have done after it mattered.\n\nYou have four years. Let’s make sure you know what to do with them.",
