        estMinutes: 6,
        isFree: true,
        body: "The first few weeks in the U.S. feel incredible.\n\nEverything is new. The campus is bigger than you imagined. The people seem confident and friendly. Your classes feel exciting. You think, “I’ve got this.”\n\nAnd then, slowly, something starts to shift.\n\nIt’s hard to explain at first. It’s not one big thing. It’s a bunch of small things hitting you at the same time. Your professor talks fast and makes jokes that everyone laughs at — and you get it, kind of, but not fully. You go back to your place after class and the silence feels different from back home. No family around. No old friends to call. You eat alone more than you expected.\n\nAnd then the thought creeps in:\n\n> “Is something wrong with me?”\n\nI’m here to tell you: no. There isn’t.\n\nWhat you’re feeling is normal. Almost every international student goes through this exact same thing. The problem isn’t that you’re struggling. The problem is that nobody warned you it was coming — and nobody taught you how to deal with it.\n\n## It’s Not Just the Language\n\nA lot of people think the hardest part of being an international student is the English.\n\nIt’s not.\n\nYour English is probably fine. Good enough to be here, at least. The real difficulty is everything happening at the same time.\n\nYou’re in a new country. New system. New culture. New rules — written and unwritten. You don’t fully understand the jokes. You’re not sure how to act in certain situations. You feel like everyone around you already knows something you don’t.\n\nAnd while all of that is happening, you’re also trying to keep up with classes, figure out where things are, and pretend you’re doing okay.\n\nThat’s a lot. And it’s okay to admit that it’s a lot.\n\nThe students who struggle most aren’t the ones who find it hard. They’re the ones who pretend it’s not hard and then get swallowed by it quietly.\n\n## The Freeze\n\nHere’s what happens to most international students in their first months:\n\n> They freeze.\n\nNot all at once. Gradually. Quietly.\n\nIn class, the professor asks a question. You know the answer — or at least you think you do. But you’re not sure if your English will come out right. You’re worried about your accent. You’re afraid of looking dumb in front of everyone. So you stay quiet.\n\nOne time becomes twice. Twice becomes a habit. By week four, you haven’t said a single word in class, and it feels way too late to start now.\n\nOutside of class, the same thing happens. You go to an event, stand near the wall for twenty minutes, feel awkward, and leave. You tell yourself, “Next time I’ll talk to people.” But next time you do the same thing. And then, without realizing it, you’ve built a wall around yourself. You go to class. You go home. You talk to your family back home. You watch shows in your language. You survive — but you’re not really here. I did parts of this myself. Most people do. The question isn’t whether you’ll feel this way. The question is whether you let it run for too long.\n\n## The Comfort Trap\n\nOne of the first things you’ll do when you arrive is find people from your country.\n\nAnd I get it. It’s natural. When everything around you is unfamiliar, finding someone who speaks your language, who gets your references, who doesn’t make you feel like a foreigner — that’s a relief. There’s nothing wrong with that. Those friendships can be real and valuable.\n\nBut here’s what I need you to be careful about: if those are the only people you spend time with, you’re going to slow yourself down without even realizing it.\n\nAnd it’s not just about who you hang out with. It’s about the routine you fall into. Class. Gym. Cafeteria. Dorm. Class. Gym. Cafeteria. Dorm. Repeat. It feels like you’re living your life, but really you’re just moving through a loop — the same four places, the same few people, day after day. Safe. Predictable. And completely limiting.\n\nYour English stops growing because you don’t practice it. Your social circle stays small because you don’t meet new people. And when an opportunity comes up — a club, an event, a chance to meet someone who could open a door for you — you miss it because you’re always with the same group, in the same place,\n\nhaving the same conversations.\n\n> The bubble feels safe. That’s exactly why it’s dangerous.\n\n## The Comparison Game\n\nHere’s another thing that will mess with your head:\n\nYou’re going to look around and feel like everyone else already has it figured out.\n\nThey already have friend groups. They already know how everything works. They already seem comfortable. They’re laughing, they’re relaxed, they’re involved in things.\n\nAnd you’re still trying to figure out how to print something at the library.\n\nHere’s the truth: most of them are just as lost as you are. They’re just better at hiding it. Stop measuring your beginning against someone else’s middle.\n\n## What You Should Actually Do\n\nOkay. Enough about the problem. Let’s talk about what to do about it.\n\nAccept that it’s going to be uncomfortable.\n\nThis is the most important one. If you go into this expecting it to feel easy, every hard moment will feel like a sign that something is wrong. If you go in expecting it to be uncomfortable — because it will be — you’ll handle it better. Discomfort is not a warning. It’s part of the process.\n\nShow up, even when you don’t feel ready.\n\nSpeak in class, even with an accent. Say hi to the person next to you, even if your English isn’t perfect. Go to the event, even if you feel awkward. You don’t need to be smooth. You just need to show up. And your accent? That’s not a flaw. That’s proof. Proof that you learned a whole other language, packed your bags, and showed up somewhere most people would never have the courage to go. The ones who make you feel small for how you sound? They only speak one language. Remember that.\n\nTalk to at least one new person every week.\n\nIt doesn’t have to be a deep conversation. It can be as simple as asking a classmate what they thought of the lecture. Small interactions build familiarity. Familiarity builds comfort. Comfort builds connection. Start small. And go alone if you have to.\n\nCreate a simple routine.\n\nOne of the quietest ways that the first months can destroy you is through chaos. No structure, no rhythm, no anchor. Build a simple daily structure — when you wake up, when you study, when you get out of the house. It doesn’t need to be rigid. It just needs to exist.\n\nAnd don’t isolate.\n\nEven on the days when you really don’t feel like it, get out of your room. Go to the library, a café, the campus center — anywhere that puts you around people. Isolation is quiet, but it hits hard. The longer you stay in it, the harder it becomes to get out.\n\n## One Last Thing\n\nThese first months are not about being great.\n\nThey’re not about having the perfect social life, getting perfect grades, or figuring out your entire future. They’re about one thing: not freezing.\n\nIf you can push through the discomfort, speak when it’s awkward, show up when you don’t feel like it, and ask for help when you need it — you’re already doing better than most people.\n\nBecause most people freeze.\n\nAnd the ones who freeze at the beginning spend a long time trying to catch up later.\n\nYou don’t have to be that person.",
