        estMinutes: 7,
        body: "Back in high school, there was always someone keeping track of you.\n\nA teacher reminding you about the test on Friday. A counselor checking if you were on track to graduate. A parent asking if you did your homework. The system was built around making sure you didn’t fall through the cracks.\n\n> College in the U.S. is nothing like that.\n\nNobody is going to remind you about your deadlines. Nobody is going to warn you that you picked a terrible professor. Nobody is going to stop you from making a decision that costs you an entire semester. And nobody is going to come find you when you’re falling behind.\n\nThat’s not because the system is bad. American college actually has incredible resources — advising, office hours, tutoring, career centers, academic support. It’s genuinely good.\n\nBut here’s the catch: it only works if you go use it.\n\nEverything here runs on one thing: autonomy. You are in charge. And if you don’t take that seriously from day one, the system won’t fail you dramatically. It’ll just quietly let you make every mistake on your own.\n\n## The Syllabus Is Not a Formality\n\nFirst week of class. The professor hands out the syllabus — or posts it online. You glance at it, think “I’ll read this properly later,” and move on.\n\nLater never really comes.\n\nThen week six arrives and you realize you missed an assignment that was worth 15% of your grade. Or you show up to class on a random Tuesday and find out there’s a midterm today. Or you spend the whole semester thinking a class is graded one way — and it isn’t.\n\nAll of that information was in the syllabus. On page one. On day one.\n\nThe syllabus is not a formality. It’s the map of the entire class.\n\nIt tells you every deadline, every exam, every rule, how your grade is calculated, and what the professor actually expects from you. Ignoring it is like getting on a road trip without looking at the directions and just hoping you end up in the right place.\n\nRead it in the first week. Every single one. Put every important date into your calendar the same day. It takes thirty minutes and it will save you from a lot of unnecessary stress.\n\n## The Professor Matters More Than the Class\n\nHere’s something most freshmen don’t realize until it’s too late:\n\n> The same class can be a completely different experience depending on who teaches it.\n\nOne professor is clear, organized, engaging. They explain things well, give you fair exams, and actually seem to care whether you learn. The other teaches the same subject but is confusing, disorganized, and impossible to follow. Same class. Same credits. Completely different reality.\n\nPicking a class without researching the professor is like buying a flight without checking the airline. The destination is the same — but the experience might be miserable.\n\nBefore you register for anything, look up the professor. Use RateMyProfessors. Ask students who already took the class. Ask upperclassmen. Spend ten minutes doing your homework before committing to a semester with someone who might make your life unnecessarily hard.\n\nThis one habit alone can change the quality of your entire semester.\n\n## You Need to Understand Your Own Degree\n\nThis one surprises a lot of people.\n\nYou can spend two years going to class, passing exams, and doing everything that feels right — and still be behind on graduating without even knowing it.\n\nWhy? Because most students don’t actually understand their own degree requirements.\n\nHow many credits do you need to graduate? Which classes are required for your major? What counts as an elective? What’s the difference between a major, a minor, and a general education requirement? Which required classes are only offered once a year?\n\nIf you can’t answer those questions right now, that’s a problem — and it’s a more common problem than you think. Students find out junior or senior year that they’re missing a required class that won’t be offered again for twelve months. Just like that, graduation gets pushed back.\n\nDon’t let that be you. Sit down with your degree plan early. Understand exactly where you are and where you need to go. And if anything is unclear — which it probably will be — go talk to your academic advisor. That’s literally what they’re there for.\n\n## Office Hours Are an Advantage, Not a Last Resort\n\nMost students only go to office hours when they’re desperate.\n\nThey’re failing. The exam is in two days. They don’t understand anything. And now they show up to the professor’s office hoping to fix weeks of confusion in one session.\n\nThat’s not how you should be using office hours.\n\nOffice hours are an advantage. They’re your chance to ask questions before you’re lost, to show the professor that you actually care, and to build a real relationship with someone who can help you — not just in that class, but beyond it. Professors remember the students who show up. They write recommendation letters. They mention names to colleagues. They open doors.\n\nAnd honestly? For you as an international student, office hours are even more valuable. If something wasn’t clear in class — the way it was explained, the cultural reference you didn’t catch, the context that went over your head — office hours is where you fix that. One on one, no pressure, no audience.\n\nGo early. Go before you need to. Even if your only question is small. It matters more than you think.\n\n## Your College Portal Is Your Control Center\n\nGrades. Payments. Financial aid. Holds. Deadlines. Class registration. Transcripts.\n\nIt’s all in your college portal.\n\nAnd ignoring it is one of the most common ways students create problems for themselves without realizing it. You miss a payment deadline and suddenly there’s a hold on your account that stops you from registering for next semester’s classes. You don’t check your email and miss an important announcement from your advisor. You wait until registration opens to figure out what classes you’re taking — and everything you wanted is already full.\n\nCheck your portal regularly. Not once a month. Not when something feels wrong. Regularly. Make it a habit the same way you check your phone. Because in college, the information you need is almost always already there — you just have to look.\n\n## What You Should Actually Do\n\nNone of this is complicated. It just requires you to be intentional from the start.\n\n◆ Read every syllabus in your first week. All of them. Put the important dates in your calendar that same day.\n\n◆ Research your professors before registering. RateMyProfessors exists for a reason. Ask people who took the class.\n\n◆ Learn your degree requirements early. Know exactly what you need to graduate, what’s required, and what you have left.\n\n◆ Meet your academic advisor at the start of each semester. Not when something goes wrong. Before things go wrong.\n\n◆ Go to office hours at least once per class in the first weeks. You don’t need a big question. Just go. ◆ Check your portal every week. Holds, deadlines, grades, announcements. Make it a habit.\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nHere’s the honest truth about college in the U.S.:\n\n> The system is not designed to catch you when you fall. It’s designed to give you everything you need — and then get out of your way.\n\nThat’s actually a good thing, if you know how to use it. You have more freedom and more resources here than most students in the world. But that only works in your favor if you’re paying attention. The students who understand this early move faster, stress less, and make better decisions.\n\nThe ones who figure it out later spend half their time putting out fires that were completely avoidable. Learn the game. Play it smart. From day one.",
