        estMinutes: 6,
        body: "There’s a version of college where you work hard, show up to class, do everything you’re supposed to do — and still end up behind.\n\nNot because you weren’t trying. But because of who you were surrounded by.\n\nI know that sounds harsh. But it’s one of the most honest things I can tell you, and almost nobody says it out loud.\n\nThe people around you in college will shape you more than any class, any professor,\n\n> or any book.\n\nThey’ll influence what you care about, what you think is possible, and what opportunities you even know exist. And most of that happens without you noticing.\n\n## The Information You’re Missing\n\nHere’s something about college that takes most people too long to realize:\n\nA lot of the most important information never shows up in class.\n\nIt shows up in conversations. Someone tells you which professor to avoid. Someone mentions an internship that just opened up. Someone says “you should apply to this, the deadline is next week.” Someone warns you about a class that looks easy but destroys your GPA.\n\nThat’s real information. The kind that changes your semester — or your year.\n\nAnd if you’re surrounded by people who don’t know these things, or don’t care about them, you simply never hear about them.\n\nYour social circle is not just about friendship. It’s about the information you have access to. It’s about the standards you hold yourself to. It’s about what feels normal and what feels possible.\n\nChoose it carefully.\n\n## The Comfortable but Going Nowhere Circle\n\nHere’s a trap that’s harder to see than you’d think.\n\nYou’re around good people. People you genuinely like. You hang out, you laugh, you have fun. But nobody in the group is really moving anywhere. No one talks about internships, opportunities, or what they’re building. No ambition, no sense of direction.\n\nAnd slowly, without realizing it, you start to match that energy.\n\nYou stop pushing yourself as much. You stop looking for opportunities. You start thinking that where you are is fine — because everyone around you seems to think so too.\n\nThat’s not a bad friendship. It’s just not enough.\n\nThe problem with comfortable circles is that they don’t challenge you. And four years go by feeling pleasant — but when you look back, you realize you played it small. Not because you weren’t capable. Because nobody around you was pushing you to be more.\n\n## The Person Ahead of You\n\nThen one day, you meet someone different.\n\nA junior. A senior. Someone who’s already done what you’re trying to figure out. And they casually say something like: “Oh, if you want to do X, you should start building Y now. And avoid that class — take this one instead with Professor Z.”\n\n> In thirty seconds, they just gave you information that would have taken you a year to discover on your own.\n\nThat’s what being around the right people looks like. Not someone telling you what to do with your life. Just someone who’s a few steps ahead, willing to share what they’ve learned.\n\nThat person is on your campus right now. You just have to find them.\n\n## The Three Types of People You Need\n\nI want to make this simple. In college, you need three types of people around you — and if one is missing, you’ll feel it.\n\nFriends who keep you grounded.\n\nPeople who make your life better. Who you can be yourself with. Who are there for the real stuff, not just the good times.\n\nPeople at the same phase as you.\n\nGoing through the same things at the same time. You can figure things out together, hold each other accountable, and grow side by side. There’s something powerful about that.\n\nPeople ahead of you.\n\nUpperclassmen, seniors, alumni — people who have already walked the road you’re on. They know things you don’t. They’ve made the mistakes you’re about to make. If you’re smart, you get close to them early and listen carefully.\n\nAll three matter. And you have to be intentional about building all three.\n\n## How to Actually Get Close to People\n\nA lot of students understand this in theory but freeze in practice.\n\nThey see the senior they want to talk to and think: “What do I even say? Why would they want to talk to me? I have nothing to offer.”\n\nHere’s the truth: most people, especially upperclassmen, are happy to talk about their experience. They went through what you’re going through. They remember how confusing it was. And most of them will help — if you ask the right way.\n\nNot vague. Not lazy. Specific and direct.\n\nDon’t just say “Can I pick your brain sometime?” That goes nowhere. Instead, try something like: “Hey, I saw you’re in the finance club — I’m a freshman thinking about that path. Would you mind if I asked you a few questions about how you got started?”\n\nThat’s it. Specific, respectful, easy to say yes to.\n\n## Real Relationships vs. Fake Networking\n\nThere’s a version of “networking” that everyone can see through. The person who only shows up when they need something. Who’s suddenly friendly when there’s an opportunity involved. Who treats every interaction like a transaction.\n\nDon’t be that person. People notice. And it closes more doors than it opens.\n\nReal relationships take time. You don’t build them in one conversation. You build them by showing up consistently, being genuinely interested, following through on things you say you’ll do, and caring about the other person — not just what they can do for you.\n\n## What You Should Actually Do\n\n◆ Find at least one upperclassman to learn from. Just someone a couple of years ahead who seems to know what they’re doing. Ask them one good question.\n\n◆ Pay attention to the energy of the people around you. Are they moving forward? Do they talk about goals, opportunities, growth?\n\n◆ Go to events and spaces where driven people gather. The right environment puts the right people in front of you.\n\n◆ Ask specific questions. Vague questions get vague answers. Specific questions start real conversations.\n\n◆ Be the kind of person others want to help. Show up. Follow through. Listen well. Say thank you.\n\n## Build It on Purpose\n\nMost people build their social circle by accident. Whoever was in their dorm. Whoever sat next to them in class. Whoever happened to be around.\n\nAnd there’s nothing wrong with starting there. But don’t stay there by accident.\n\nBe intentional. Look around and ask yourself honestly: are the people closest to me pushing me forward? Are they people I’m learning from? Are they people I respect?\n\n> Your environment shapes you more than your intentions ever will. Build it on purpose.",
